---
import {withBase} from '../lib/paths.mjs';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout title="Take Folklore into Codex — Folklore"><article class="prose-page"><h1>The field guide,<br/>where you build.</h1><p class="lede">An offline catalog of 30 UI elements and 10 visual styles. Bring the vocabulary, anatomy, and implementation notes into Codex.</p><div class="download-options"><a href={withBase('/downloads/folklore-plugin-1.2.0.zip')} download>Download plugin<span>Codex package · v1.2.0 · ZIP</span></a><a href={withBase('/downloads/folklore-skill-1.2.0.zip')} download>Download standalone skill<span>Portable SKILL.md · v1.2.0 · ZIP</span></a></div><h2>Install in Codex</h2><p>Run these commands in your terminal:</p><pre><code>codex plugin marketplace add Parthawe/folklore-ui
codex plugin add folklore@folklore-local</code></pre><p>Start a new Codex chat, then try:</p><pre><code>Use $folklore to identify “the dark layer behind a popup” and help me implement it in this project.</code></pre><p>Folklore identifies a scrim, explains its dim layer, optional blur, and dismiss area, and checks the implementation against your project.</p><h2>Install the standalone skill</h2><ol><li>Download and extract the skill archive. It contains a folder named <code>folklore</code>.</li><li>For a project, place that folder in <code>.agents/skills/</code> at the repository root. For personal use, place it in <code>~/.agents/skills/</code>.</li><li>Start a new Codex session and invoke <code>$folklore</code>. Node.js 18 or newer is needed for the lookup commands; the Markdown references can be read without it.</li></ol><p>Example request:</p><pre><code>Use $folklore to name the dark layer behind my popup, explain its parts, and help me build it in this project.</code></pre><h2>Use the plugin package</h2><p>The plugin bundles the same skill with a Codex compatibility manifest. Extract it into a local plugin source, add it to a local or repository marketplace, and install from that source in a supporting Codex desktop surface. This download is not a listing in the public plugin directory.</p><p><a href="https://developers.openai.com/plugins/build/plugins">Current OpenAI plugin installation and packaging guidance</a> explains the marketplace format and supported surfaces. The repository includes a marketplace example in <code>examples/marketplace.json</code>.</p><h2>Look things up locally</h2><p>From the extracted <code>folklore</code> skill directory:</p><pre><code>node scripts/lookup.mjs search "gray shapes before content loads"
node scripts/lookup.mjs get skeleton
node scripts/lookup.mjs compare skeleton spinner
node scripts/lookup.mjs search "frosted cards" --kind style</code></pre><h2>Audit a screen for design slop</h2><p>The plugin also includes <code>$design-slop-audit</code>. It reviews visible design choices and interaction states, with evidence and concrete fixes.</p><pre><code>Use $design-slop-audit to review this screen. Flag generic layout choices, filler copy, weak hierarchy, and incomplete states. Give specific fixes and preserve the parts that work.</code></pre><p>Attach a screenshot, share a URL, or point Codex at your project. The audit does not guess whether AI made the screen.</p><p><a href={withBase('/downloads/design-slop-audit-1.2.0.zip')} download>Download the standalone screen-audit skill</a>.</p><h2>Prepare a task-aware design review</h2><p>The screen-audit skill now includes a local review planner and a curated primary-source research guide. From its extracted folder:</p><pre><code>node scripts/review.mjs --task "Help a customer review their order and pay" --screen "An animated background, fake testimonials, and no error state" --markdown</code></pre><p>The planner prepares risks and checks from your description. It does not inspect a screen or call a model. Use the exported brief with the actual screen or project in Codex.</p><h2>What the skill does</h2><p>It identifies likely patterns, distinguishes neighboring concepts, explains anatomy and platform vocabulary, and adapts build or debug briefs to your project. Search scores rank matches; they are not confidence percentages.</p><h2>Versioned, inspectable, offline</h2><p>No API key, account, or hosted service is required. Installation does not send project files to Folklore. The host coding agent continues to operate under its own data and permission settings. Updating the catalog means installing a newer release.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/Parthawe/folklore-ui">Read the source and release notes on GitHub</a>.</p></article></Layout>
